32:\section*{2. Preliminaries}
34:\subsection*{2.1. Problem setting and approach}
36:We consider a simulator with parameters $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ (potentially nonparametric) which stochastically generates samples $\boldsymbol{x}$ from its implicit likelihood $p(\boldsymbol{x} \mid \boldsymbol{\theta})$. After having observed data $\boldsymbol{x}_{o}$, we aim to infer the posterior distribution $p\left(\boldsymbol{\theta} \mid \boldsymbol{x}_{o}\right)$ of parameters given data, but also retain the flexibility to capture any other conditional of the full joint $p(\boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{x})$. We, therefore, introduce the joint $\hat{\boldsymbol{x}}=(\boldsymbol{\theta}, \boldsymbol{x})$, that will serve as input for a transformer together with a mask indicating which values are observed. The transformer will then use attention mechanisms to compute the corresponding sequence of output scores of equal size. The scores corresponding to unobserved variables will then form the basis of a diffusion model representing the distribution over these variables. We first give background on the main ingredients (transformers and score-based diffusion models) in this section before giving a detailed description in Sec. 3.
